          %p Note that “kind of directed graph” is a used in a loose, undefined sense, sufficiently clear from the context.
          %h2#bidirected_graphs Bidirected graphs
          %p
            Roughly speaking, bidirected graphs are quivers in which
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                each non-loop arrow gets decorated with
                %em two
                elements drawn from a two-element set fixed in advanced, one thought to be attached to the arrow’s tail, the other to its head
            %em sometimes
            interpreted to be
            %em signs
            and equipped with arithmetical structure (essentially, the two-element set is taken to be a two-element
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
          %p
            (The treatment of
            %em loops
            is slightly controversial in the combinatorical literature, prompting some ad-hoc-ery, and this is explicitly commented on at least once in the literature.) The category-theoretic literature seems not to have picked up on this so far, and could clarify the issue.
          %p
            Bidirected graphs are important in the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flows"} flows
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphs"} graphs
          %h2#connections_to_category_theory Connections to category theory
          %p Herein some evident aspects are pointed out explicitly.
          %h3#underlying_quivers Underlying quivers
          %p
            A basic connection is that for any category, its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
            is a directed graph.
          %p
            Another aspect is that a
            %em nonempty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/digraph"} digraph
            never is isomorphic to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} underlying
            any category: such a quiver
            %em always
            has a loop at each vertex, which is already enough to rule out any isomorphism.
          %h3#an_application_of_directed_graphs_to_category_theory An application of directed graphs to category theory
          %p
            Briefly, and from a slightly
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+theory"} model theoretical
            point of view, the charm of this application is that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+scheme"} A. J. Power's pasting theorem
            is an example of a
            %em transfer
            of knowledge from a
            %em
              theory without
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature+%28in+logic%29"} function symbols
            to a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                theory with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature+%28in+logic%29"} function symbols
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/digraphs"} digraphs
            = succeed "," do
              %em purely relational
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            makes essential use of several
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature+%28in+logic%29"} function symbols
          %p
            An example of a use of digraph theory in category theory is giving a rigorous justification of the notational practice of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+diagrams"} pasting diagrams
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Johnson1987"} Johnson 1987
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Johnson1989"} Johnson 1989
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Power1990"} Power 1990
          %h3#all_quivers_deemed_irreflexive_by_lawvere
            %em All
            quivers deemed irreflexive by Lawvere
          %p
            There is an article of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
            which is relevant in one precise respect to a disambiguation page such as the present one:
            = succeed "." do
              %em an unusual use of the adjective “irreflexive”
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GeneralizedGraphs"} Lawvere 1989
          %blockquote
            %p
              [..] the elementary “parallel process”
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo =
                    %mo •
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E= \bullet\overset{\rightarrow}{\rightarrow}\bullet
              is a reflexive graph which happens to admit only one definition of composition making it into a category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{P}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actions"} actions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi P
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{\mathbf{P}^{\mathrm{op}}}
              are the
              %strong irreflexive
              graphs (the negative is in a way appropriate even for those objects which happen to have loops at some point
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              %em any
              two such loops).
          %p
            With “the negative is in a way appropriate” Lawvere explains the use of the strong negation
            = succeed "," do
              %em irr-
            %em some
            of its vertices, which is obviously different from what one would expect from the usual sense of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreflexive+relation"} irreflexive relation
          %p
            Some authors have adopted this interesting variant sense of “irreflexive”, prompting an additional use of the a modifier “strict” to form a composite “strict irreflexive graph”. (cf. (
            %a{:href => "#GraphsOfMorphismsOfGraphs"}> Brown et al 2008
            ), which uses “digraph” as a synonym for “strict irreflexive graph”, and this sense of “digraph” is precisely the one in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/digraph"} digraph
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/digraph"} digraph
            is a “strictly-Lawvere-irreflexive graph”.
          %p
            From a graph-theoretical point of view, every digraph is a quiver, but not every quiver is a digraph (again, in the sense of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DG2nd"} Gutin and Bang-Jensen 2009
